WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.510
Continuing in this vein of wonder, we
have Robert-Houdin. This is the

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:08.069
gentleman who revealed the defecating
duck to be a fake. So he did many things.

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:13.019
Not only was he an incredible clock
maker and magician and also invented the

00:00:13.019 --> 00:00:16.440
mystery clock, he actually was hired by
the French government to put down a

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.090
rebellion.
So his magic was so good they hired him

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:25.890
to go out and pretend to be a sorcerer,
and he basically saved France. So not

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:30.630
many clock makers can put that on their
CV. What's most interesting is the fact

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.710
that Robert-Houdin was the son of a
watchmaker, and had planned to be a

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.670
watchmaker, but actually went to law
school. And while he went to a book shop

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:43.079
to collect a two-volume set on law, he
was mistakenly given a two-volume set on

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:49.739
magic. And the world was never the same.
He ended up moving into Paris and

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:54.110
marrying the daughter of a prominent
clock maker. And his time there really

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:58.440
totally redid magic.
He's considered the father of modern

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.789
magic, and was the first magician to
incorporate mechanical devices and

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:06.840
clocks using electricity into his shows,
which was definitely a result of his

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.880
work as a clock maker. So not only did Houdin inspire a lot of firms to

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.470
actually go on and develop other mystery
clocks, but he also inspired the very

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.020
famous Russian goldsmith Peter Carl
Fabergé.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.570
So, Fabergé was the goldsmith to the Czar,
Nicholas II, and took much of his

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:28.590
inspiration from master works of the
past. So this is his version of the

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.570
Houdin Orange Tree. This is known as the
Bay Tree Egg, this is from 1911. So

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.350
instead of having the handkerchief that
carries this piece with the butterflies,

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:43.049
it actually has a singing bird in the
center, which is also a homage to

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:46.790
Jaquet-Droz, who invented the Singing Bird.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:54.030
Here is none other than his tiny Silver
Swan automaton. This actually came in

00:01:54.030 --> 00:02:00.119
another one of his beautiful eggs.
He actually had 52 of these eggs that

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:05.100
were created by Faberge for the Czar
Nicholas II. He had a standing order of

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:10.319
two of these every year, so there was one
for his mother and one for his wife. This

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:15.150
beautiful egg has matte lilac enamel,
it has the swan that sits in a bed of

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.480
aquamarine with all these beautiful
little flowers. What's probably the most

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.720
surprising thing about this mechanical
swan is the fact that it is actually

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:33.390
made of gold and then plated in
silver. How's that for an Easter present?

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:45.190
And this is from the year 1906. When
Peter Carl took over his father's firm,

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:49.630
there was definitely this move from
producing jewelry to producing kind of

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:57.010
artist-jeweler type objects. So this
particular one was the Peacock Egg. Don't

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:00.910
know if I mentioned this, but his mother
got all of the mechanical ones, so this

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:05.680
was this beautiful peacock that
certainly had to have been influenced by

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.270
another older artwork. Since the Fabergé
firm was actually also the keepers of

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.810
the objects inside the Hermitage, they
looked after and repaired many of the

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:18.850
objects within the collection there. This
allowed Fabergé to go and study all of

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:24.310
these older pieces and build replicas and different pieces that would pay

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:30.770
homage to these master works of the past.

